salt
{
  <b>ORE STUDIO - SYSTEM ADMINISTRATION: OPERATIONS</b>
  --
  <b>SEE THE RUNNING SERVICES</b>
  --
  {
    Updated 14:32:05 | [ Refresh ] | Watching: every 30 s
  }
  --
  (Version skew: ores.compute.service runs v0.0.24 while the rest run v0.0.25)
  --
  {#
    Service | Instances | Instance | Status | Version | Last heartbeat
    ores.compute.service | (1 of 2) | 6f2c19a1 | running | v0.0.25 | 4 s ago
    | | c47e5508 | running | v0.0.24 (older build) | 21 s ago
    ores.iam.service | 2 of 2 | 91b0f33d | running | v0.0.25 | 12 s ago
    | | 4d2ab8e0 | running | v0.0.25 | 9 s ago
    ores.reporting.service | (1 of 2) | 2a9977f6 | (stopped) | v0.0.25 | 5 m ago
    | | - | (missing) | - | -
    ores.telemetry.service | 2 of 2 | 8e31c0f4 | running | v0.0.25 | 2 s ago
    | | 9f42d1a7 | running | v0.0.25 | 6 s ago
  }
  --
  Summary: (16 of 19) instances reported in the last five minutes | (1 stopped) | (2 missing)
  --
  Read-only. One row per instance the registry expects, whether it reports or
  not; the rows never disappear. The state words are the installation's own;
  running is reported in the last five minutes. The version is the release the
  instance sends with its heartbeat, and an older build is marked where it
  appears. The compute wrappers are the grid screen's: a wrapper runs on a
  node, and it is shown with the node.
  --
  <b>NOTHING REPORTED IN THE LAST FIVE MINUTES</b>
  --
  Every expected instance is missing. The installation may be starting, or the
  services may have stopped. The screen keeps every row and cannot say why;
  the state has no read of its own yet.
}

